<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chahamanas of Naddula</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1197 CE (approx.) · capital Naddula</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chauhan branch of Marwar ruling from Nadol, offshoot of the Ajmer dynasty and forerunner of the Chauhans of Jalor.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 950 Lakshmana, a younger member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a>, carved out a kingdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadol">Naddula</a>, modern Nadol in the Pali district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. Inscriptions and later bardic tradition, where he appears as Rao Lakhan, record the founding.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadol">Naddula</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> held the southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jodhpur-state">Marwar</a> plains and the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the north. They fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/paramara-dynasty">Paramara dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a>, and from the 12th century often served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukyas</a> as feudatories; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhana">Alhana</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kelhana">Kelhana</a> fought in Chaulukya campaigns. The kings and their nobles supported both Shaiva and Jain temples, and Nadol has many Jain shrines.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb al-Din Aibak</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadol">Nadol</a> in 1197, ending the main line. A branch founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipala">Kirtipala</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhana">Alhana</a>, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a>, where the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chahamanas-of-jalor">Songara Chauhans</a> ruled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> took the fort in 1311.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 950 CE – 1197 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.61%;width:4.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1197 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naddula</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lakshmana, Alhana, Kelhana</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Apabhramsha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chauhans of Nadol</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1074&amp;focus=chahamanas-of-naddula" aria-label="Chahamanas of Naddula on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6808 1358 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chahamanas of Naddula lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7128 1614h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7128 1614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=1074&amp;focus=chahamanas-of-naddula">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
